  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 208 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nGenerally quiet weather is expected through Thursday with upper\nlevel ridging in place across the western Great Lakes. The main\nexception may be across northern Wisconsin this afternoon as the \nLake Superior lake breeze sinks south with a small (10-30%) chance\nof showers and thunderstorms this afternoon. A few pop-up storms \nare also possible on Thursday as well (10-20% chance); however, no\nstrong storms are expected during this period. Models indicate \nsome smoke could reach the surface at times starting this \nafternoon and tonight across central and north-central Wisconsin \nthen across the entire region Thursday into Thursday night. This \nsmoke isn't expected to be nearly as bad as earlier this month, \nwith some minor visibility restrictions and a drop in air quality \ninto the moderate or yellow category expected.\n\nA potent shortwave and surface low will bring a better chance \n(60-80%) for showers and thunderstorms on Friday into Friday\nnight, with light activity possibly lingering into Saturday.\nDespite decent SBCAPEs of 1-2 J/g across the north, bulk shear\nvalues are paltry below 20 knots; therefore, severe storms are not\nanticipated. However, PWATs around 1.75 inches along with long and\nskinny CAPE profiles and decent forcing indicate there is a\nmarginal risk for excessive rainfall (level 1 of 4) across much of\nthe area, with isolated areas in central Wisconsin at a slight\nrisk (level 2 of 4).\n\nHigh pressure building in behind the departing low portends a\ndrier forecast for the second half of the weekend, lasting into\nthe early portion of next week.",
